QB.add(39,[{"n":1,"q":"A 60-year-old male patient presents to his family physician with complaints of fatigue, malaise, and unintentional weight loss for the past 6 months. On examination, the patient appears pale, and there is splenomegaly. The blood picture reveals anaemia, leukocytosis, and basophilia. Upon suspicion, genetic testing was done, and a diagnosis was made. Which of the following is the underlying genetic defect in this condition?","o":[{"t":"Translocation between chromosomes 12 and 21"},{"t":"Unbalanced translocation between the long arm of chromosomes 9 and 22"},{"t":"Deletion of chromosomes 11q, 13q, 17p"},{"t":"Balanced translocation between the long arms of chromosomes 9 and 22"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The Philadelphia chromosome is a balanced translocation between the long arm of chromosomes 9 and 22 [t(9;22)(q34.1;q11.2)] (Option B) . It is present in the majority of patients with chronic myeloid leukaemia (CML)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Formation of Philadelphia chromosome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A balanced translocation is a type of chromosomal rearrangement in which segments of two different chromosomes break and exchange places without any loss of genetic material."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"(Option D)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In the case of CML, a segment of chromosome 9 containing the ABL gene ( Abelson Leukemia) , located at the q34 region, moves to chromosome 22."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Similarly, the segment of chromosome 22 containing the BCR gene (breakpoint cluster region) , located at the q11 region, moves to chromosome 9."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This exchange results in the formation of two derivative chromosomes: one with the truncated chromosome 9 segment and the BCR-ABL fusion gene and the other with the truncated chromosome 22 segment."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The chromosome containing the BCR-ABL fusion gene is known as the Philadelphia chromosome."},{"k":"img","v":6640},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The BCR-ABL fusion forms a novel oncoprotein called p210 bcr-abl-1 (210 = molecular weight in kiloDalton—210 kDa)."},{"k":"img","v":6647},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Translocation between chromosomes 12 and 21 [t(12;21)] (Option A) is seen in B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukaemia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Deletion of chromosomes 11q, 13q, and 17p (Option C) are seen in small lymphocytic leukemia/Chronic lymphocytic leukemia."}]},{"n":2,"q":"Which of the following statements regarding the genetic basis of Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) is most accurate?","o":[{"t":"The BCR-ABL fusion gene in CML results from a translocation involving chromosomes 15 and 17."},{"t":"CML patients who progress to blast crisis often acquire mutations in the TP53 gene, leading to genomic instability."},{"t":"The BCR-ABL fusion protein activates the JAK-STAT signalling pathway, contributing to leukemic cell proliferation in CML."},{"t":"Resistance to tyrosine kinase inhibitors (TKIs) in CML is commonly associated with mutations in the BCR gene."}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct answer: B) CML patients who progress to blast crisis often acquire mutations in the TP53 gene, leading to genomic instability."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Chronic-phase CML can progress to blast crisis, characterized by the rapid proliferation of immature blast cells resembling acute leukaemia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This transformation is often associated with acquired mutations in the TP53 gene, which plays a critical role in regulating cell cycle checkpoints and DNA repair."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"TP53 mutations can lead to genomic instability, allowing for the uncontrolled growth of leukemic cells, as seen in the blast crisis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Key genetic abnormalities in CML:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Genetic Abnormality Description"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Philadelphia A reciprocal translocation between chromosomes 9 and Chromosome (Ph) 22 [t(9;22)] , resulting in the fusion of the BCR gene on"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"chromosome 22 with the ABL gene on chromosome 9 (Option A ruled out)."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"BCR-ABL Fusion Gene The fusion of BCR and ABL genes leads to a"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"constitutively active tyrosine kinase (BCR-ABL) that promotes uncontrolled cell proliferation and survival in CML."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"ABL Kinase Domain Mutations within the ABL kinase domain, particularly Mutations T315I, are associated with resistance to tyrosine kinase"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"inhibitors (TKIs) used in CML treatment. (Option D ruled out)."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"JAK-STAT Signaling Although not directly activated by BCR-ABL, JAK-STAT Pathway pathway dysregulation may contribute to CML"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"pathogenesis, including disease progression and resistance. (Option C ruled out)."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"TP53 Mutations Acquired mutations in TP53 , common in blast crisis"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"CML, lead to genomic instability, cell cycle dysregulation, and aggressive disease behaviour."}]},{"n":3,"q":"A 55-year-old male presents with fatigue, weight loss and abdominal discomfort. On physical examination, the spleen is palpable 5 cm below the left costal margin, and there is mild tenderness in the left upper quadrant. Blood investigations reveal anaemia, leukocytosis, and basophilia. Which of the following features is not associated with this condition?","o":[{"t":"Pruritus"},{"t":"Diarrhea"},{"t":"Increased NAP score"},{"t":"Left shift in differential count"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Increased NAP score"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The given clinical scenario of a patient with fatigue, weight loss, splenomegaly, and left upper quadrant pain, along with anaemia, leukocytosis, and basophilia, suggests chronic myeloid leukaemia (CML). Increased NAP score is not seen in CML; rather, it is decreased."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical features of CML:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Age group 55-65 years or older."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Anemia Fatigue, malaise, weight loss (10% in 6 months)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Splenomegaly Early satiety, left upper quadrant pain/mass."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Age group 55-65 years or older."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Thrombotic or hyperviscosity-related symptoms Priapism"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Cardiovascular complications Visual disturbances Dyspnea Pulmonary insufficiency Cerebrovascular complications"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Bleeding diathesis Retinal hemorrhage"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Gastrointestinal hemorrhage"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Progression to accelerated or blast phase Unexplained significant weight loss"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Severe fatigue Bone and joint pain Bleeding and thrombotic events"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Basophilia Causes excessive histamine production, leading to:"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Pruritus (Option A) Diarrhea (Option B) Flushing Gastrointestinal ulcers"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Neutrophil Alkaline phosphatase (NAP) score:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"NAP score is decreased in CML (Option C)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normal score: 40-100."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"NAP score is the measure of killing power of neutrophil."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A decreased NAP score results in an increased risk of infections."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Decreased NAP score Increased NAP score"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH) Stress Myelodysplastic syndrome Infection CML Leukemoid reaction"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Pregnancy Other myeloproliferative disorders except CML"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"A left shift in the differential leukocyte count (Option D) refers to an increase in the number of immature white blood cells, particularly neutrophil precursors such as bands, metamyelocytes, myelocytes, and sometimes even myeloblasts, in the peripheral blood."}]},{"n":4,"q":"A 60-year-old male presents with fatigue, malaise, weight loss, and swelling in the left upper quadrant of the abdomen. On evaluation, he was diagnosed with chronic myeloid leukaemia. Which of the following peripheral smears will be seen in this patient?","o":[{"t":""},{"t":""},{"t":""},{"t":""}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct answer: A)"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation: Peripheral smear findings in chronic myeloid leukemia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Shows many mature neutrophils, some metamyelocytes, and myelocytes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Shift to left can be seen."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Cell Description"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Myeloblast Large cells with a round or oval nucleus with dark"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"cytoplasm and no granules."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Segmented neutrophil Has a nucleus with 2-5 lobes."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Myelocyte The nucleus is oval and eccentrically placed.(D-shaped"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"nuclei)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Metamyelocyte Has a kidney bean-shaped nucleus."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"College girl/garden party appearance is seen due to non-uniform appearance (every cell appears different)."},{"k":"img","v":6656},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Option B: The smear shows small round lymphocytes with scanty cytoplasm and smudge cells, suggesting chronic lymphocytic leukaemia. The cells are called smudge cells since they are partially destroyed during the smear preparation and implicate the abnormal fragility of the white blood cells."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The smear shows small round lymphocytes with scanty cytoplasm and smudge cells, suggesting chronic lymphocytic leukaemia. The cells are called smudge cells since they are partially destroyed during the smear preparation and implicate the abnormal fragility of the white bloodcells."},{"k":"img","v":6662},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Option C: The smear shows lymphoblasts with scanty cytoplasm, coarse chromatin, and no nucleoli, which are suggestive of acute lymphoblastic leukaemia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The smear shows lymphoblasts with scanty cytoplasm, coarse chromatin, and no nucleoli, which are suggestive of acute lymphoblastic leukaemia."},{"k":"img","v":6667},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Option D: The smear shows multiple Auer rods clustered within a single cell resembling a bundle of sticks. These cells are termed Faggot cells and are seen in acute myeloid leukaemia , particularly in t(15:17) aberration (acute promyelocytic leukaemia). Auer rods areneedle-like azurophilic intracytoplasmic inclusion bodies formed by the fusion of lysosomes."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The smear shows multiple Auer rods clustered within a single cell resembling a bundle of sticks. These cells are termed Faggot cells and are seen in acute myeloid leukaemia , particularly in t(15:17) aberration (acute promyelocytic leukaemia). Auer rods are needle-like azurophilic intracytoplasmic inclusion bodies formed by the fusion of lysosomes."},{"k":"img","v":6673}],"m":1},{"n":5,"q":"A 58-year-old male is diagnosed with chronic myeloid leukemia (CML). The oncologist is planning to monitor the patient’s response to treatment and check for the presence of the Philadelphia chromosome. What is the investigation of choice for confirming the diagnosis of CML and detecting the Philadelphia chromosome?","o":[{"t":"Bone marrow examination"},{"t":"FISH (Fluorescence In Situ Hybridization)"},{"t":"Cytogenetic analysis (karyotyping)"},{"t":"PCR (Polymerase Chain Reaction)"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"FISH (Fluorescence In Situ Hybridization)"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"FISH (Fluorescence In Situ Hybridization) confirms t(9:22) translocation, it helps to quantify Philadelphia chromosome-positive cells and estimate the tumor load."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnosis of Chronic Myeloid Leukemia:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"CBC Normal RBC + Elevated TLC + Elevated platelets"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Peripheral smear Left shift with predominant segmented neutrophils,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"myelocytes, and metamyelocytes. College girl/garden party appearance."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Bone marrow Increased Myeloid:Erythroid ratio. aspirate Dwarf megakaryocytes."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Sea blue histiocytes (blue-coloured cytoplasm) Pseudo Gaucher cells marrow aspirate CML\" data-author=\"Gören nahin D, Üsküdar Teke H, Karagülle M, Andnç N, Gündüz E, Innksoy S, Balin M, Akay OM \" data-hash=\"10358\" data-license=\"CC BY 2.5\" data-source=\"https://openi.nlm. nih.gov/detailedresult?img=PMC4454064_TJH-31-428-g 1&query;=pseudo%20gaucher%20cellsn=xg&req;=4&np os;=1\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"324\""},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"KJNCQu41744700618.png\" width=\"394\" />"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"The image shows Pseudo-Gaucher cells"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"NAP/LAP score Decreased"},{"k":"img","v":6679},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"FISH (IOC) (Option B)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Confirms t(9:22) translocation Analysis to quantify Philadelphia chromosome-positive cells Estimates the tumor load"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"The image shows BCR-ABL gene fusion (red + green) in fluorescence in-situ hybridization (FISH; the ABL gene is red and the BCR gene is green)."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"PCR To quantify BCR-ABL-1"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"It can be a false negative or false positive False negative: Early part of the disease"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Bone marrow examination (Option A) can help assess overall bone marrow morphology and cellularity, but it is not the most specific test for detecting the Philadelphia chromosome."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cytogenetic analysis (karyotyping) (Option C) can identify chromosomal abnormalities, including the Philadelphia chromosome, but is less sensitive and takes longer than FISH."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"PCR (Polymerase Chain Reaction) (Option D) is highly sensitive for detecting the BCR-ABL fusion gene, but FISH is generally preferred for initial diagnosis and monitoring of the Philadelphia chromosome in CML."}]},{"n":6,"q":"A 62-year-old male with a history of chronic myeloid leukemia (CML), diagnosed two years ago and currently on imatinib therapy, presents with worsening fatigue, abdominal discomfort, and early satiety. On examination, he has splenomegaly and mild pallor. Lab tests show hemoglobin 9.5 g/dL, platelet count 90,000/µL, white blood cell count 50,000/µL, and 12% blasts on peripheral smear. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Blast crisis of CML"},{"t":"Transformation to acute myeloid leukaemia (AML)"},{"t":"Accelerated phase of CML"},{"t":"Chronic phase of CML"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The given clinical presentation of a 65-year-old male, a known case of chronic myeloid leukaemia (CML) presenting with worsening fatigue, early satiety, splenomegaly, anaemia, thrombocytopenia, leukocytosis, and increased blasts is suggestive of the accelerated phase of CML."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnostic criteria for the accelerated phase of CML:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thrombocytopenia or persistent thrombocytosis non-responsive to therapy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increase in spleen size and WBC counts unresponsive to therapy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Peripheral blood basophilia >20%."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cytogenetic evidence of clonal evolution."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Blast 10-19% in blood and/or bone marrow."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Chronic myeloid leukaemia occurs in three phases:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Chronic phase (M/C) (Option D) Accelerated phase (Option C) Blast phase"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Leukocytosis: Bimodal distribution Occurs in 50% of the patients who CML transforming into with a higher proportion of mature present with increasing anaemia, AML (Option B) segmented neutrophils and thrombocytopenia, and Occurs in an average myelocytes. splenomegaly. of 3 years after the Blast cells <10%. Blast count: 10-19% . diagnosis of CML. Increased basophils and eosinophils. Basophilia >20%. Blasts on the bone Monocytosis <3%. marrow biopsy >20%."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Presence of extramedu llary blastic infiltrates."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Blastic crisis of CML (Option A) : If the blasts are more than 30% in peripheral blood or bone marrow. Extramedullary blast proliferation gives more credibility to the diagnosis of a blast crisis."}]},{"n":7,"q":"A 58-year-old male with chronic myeloid leukemia (CML) was diagnosed six months ago and is currently on 400 mg of imatinib daily. His laboratory studies show a quantitative polymerase chain reaction (qPCR) for a BCR-ABL transcript level of 0.02% on the International Scale (IS). What is the most appropriate next step in managing this patient?","o":[{"t":"Increase the dose of imatinib to 600 mg daily"},{"t":"Obtain a bone marrow biopsy and cytogenetic analysis"},{"t":"Continue the current dose of imatinib and monitor qPCR every 3 months"},{"t":"Switch to a second-generation tyrosine kinase inhibitor (TKI) such as dasatinib or nilotinib"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct answer: C) Continue the current dose of imatinib and monitor qPCR every 3 months"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"In the management of chronic myeloid leukaemia (CML) with imatinib therapy, the goal of treatment is to achieve and maintain a deep molecular response, is assessed by quantitative polymerase chain reaction (qPCR) for BCR-ABL transcript levels on the International Scale (IS)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A complete cytogenetic response refers to the absence of Ph-positive metaphases (0% Ph positivity). This is approximately equivalent to BCR-ABL1 transcripts (IS) of 1% or less."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A qPCR level of 0.1% or less on the IS is considered a major molecular response (MMR)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"While a level o f 0.01% or less is considered a deep molecular response (DMR)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"In this scenario, the patient has achieved a qPCR level of 0.02% on the IS, indicating a good response to imatinib therapy. It is important to continue the current dose of imatinib and monitor qPCR levels every 3 months to assess for any changes in response. (Option C)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Patients should be asymptomatic."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Platelet count should be <450,000/µL."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Aim for BCR-ABL1 transcripts to be undetectable by PCR."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"No cells should contain the Philadelphia chromosome.(0% ph)"},{"k":"img","v":6686},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dose escalation to 600mg in CML (Option A) is only performed when a patient is in the accelerated phase/blastic phase . Blast / accelerated phase: dose escalated to 600-800 mg/day Side effects: myelosuppression, periorbital edema , hypophosphatemia and skin rashes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Blast / accelerated phase: dose escalated to 600-800 mg/day"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Side effects: myelosuppression, periorbital edema , hypophosphatemia and skin rashes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Obtain a bone marrow biopsy and cytogenetic analysis (Option B) : These tests are typically reserved for assessing response to treatment in specific situations, such as suspected treatment failure or disease progression."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Second-generation TKIs (Option D) may be considered in cases of imatinib resistance or intolerance due to tyrosine kinase domain mutations but are not necessary for this scenario."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Blast / accelerated phase: dose escalated to 600-800 mg/day"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Side effects: myelosuppression, periorbital edema , hypophosphatemia and skin rashes"}]},{"n":8,"q":"Which of the following best describes the mechanism of action of imatinib in the treatment of chronic myeloid leukaemia (CML)?","o":[{"t":"Inhibition of BCL-2, leading to apoptosis of leukemic cells"},{"t":"Inhibition of BCL-2, leading to apoptosis of leukemic cells"},{"t":"Blockade of aberrant tyrosine kinase signaling"},{"t":"Stimulation of DNA repair mechanisms, enhancing leukemic cell survival"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct answer: C) Blockade of aberrant tyrosine kinase signaling"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation: Imatinib exerts its therapeutic effect by binding to the ATP-binding site of the BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase domain , thereby inhibiting its enzymatic activity. (Option C)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This blockade of BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase activity prevents downstream signaling cascades involved in cell proliferation, survival, and differentiation, ultimately leading to apoptosis of leukemic cells and suppression of disease progression in CML."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This fusion protein results from the Philadelphia chromosome (Ph+) , which is formed by a reciprocal translocation between chromosomes 9 and 22 [t(9;22)(q34;q11)]."},{"k":"img","v":6690},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mode of action of imatinib on BCR-ABL. ADP, adenosine diphosphate; ATP, adenosine triphosphate; P, phosphate."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Inhibition of BCL-2, leading to apoptosis of leukemic cells (Option A) : Imatinib does not directly inhibit BCL-2, an anti-apoptotic protein, but rather targets the BCR-ABL fusion protein."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Activation of tyrosine kinase activity, promoting leukemic cell proliferation (Option B) : Imatinib does not activate tyrosine kinase; rather, it inhibits it. The BCR-ABL fusion protein, a tyrosine kinase, promotes proliferation, and imatinib counteracts this effect."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Stimulation of DNA repair mechanisms, enhancing leukemic cell survival (Option D) : Imatinib does not stimulate DNA repair mechanisms but rather inhibits tyrosine kinase signaling pathways."}]},{"n":9,"q":"A 52-year-old male with chronic myeloid leukemia (CML) presents to the emergency department with severe weakness, nausea, and decreased urine output for the past 24 hours, following a new chemotherapy regimen. Lab studies show: Potassium - 6.2 mmol/L, Phosphorus - 7.8 mg/dL, Calcium - 6.5 mg/dL, uric acid - 12 mg/dL, and Creatinine - 2.2 mg/dL. What is the most appropriate initial management for this patient?","o":[{"t":"Intravenous hydration and diuretics"},{"t":"Intravenous hydration, rasburicase, and diuretics"},{"t":"Intravenous hydration and calcium gluconate"},{"t":"Intravenous hydration, loop diuretics, and sodium bicarbonate"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct answer: B) Intravenous hydration, rasburicase, and diuretics"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient's clinical presentation with laboratory findings of hyperkalemia, hyperphosphatemia, hypocalcemia, hyperuricemia, and elevated creatinine is consistent with tumour lysis syndrome (TLS)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In chronic myeloid leukaemia (CML), initiation of chemotherapy can lead to a massive release of nucleic acids, potassium, phosphorus, uric acid, and other cellular contents from lysed leukemic cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The initial management of TLS includes aggressive intravenous hydration to maintain renal perfusion and promote urinary excretion of uric acid and other metabolic byproducts. Rasburicase, a recombinant urate oxidase enzyme , is indicated to rapidly lower serum uric acid levels. It converts uric acid into a more soluble metabolite, allantoin, which is easily excreted by the kidneys. Diuretics may also be administered to enhance urinary excretion of electrolytes and prevent renal damage. Prophylaxis for TLS is Allopurinol- it reduces the risk of uric acid nephropathy but is of no benefit once tumor lysis has occurred Rasburicase is contraindicated in patients with glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency who are unable to break down hydrogen peroxide, an end product of the urate oxidase reaction. Lysis Syndrome\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"10362\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"1000\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/LhuUEYxLjBR md3bRZpr71744701330.png\" width=\"667\" />"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Rasburicase, a recombinant urate oxidase enzyme , is indicated to rapidly lower serum uric acid levels. It converts uric acid into a more soluble metabolite, allantoin, which is easily excreted by the kidneys."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diuretics may also be administered to enhance urinary excretion of electrolytes and prevent renal damage."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prophylaxis for TLS is Allopurinol- it reduces the risk of uric acid nephropathy but is of no benefit once tumor lysis has occurred"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Rasburicase is contraindicated in patients with glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency who are unable to break down hydrogen peroxide, an end product of the urate oxidase reaction. Lysis Syndrome\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"10362\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"1000\""},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"width=\"667\" />"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Rasburicase, a recombinant urate oxidase enzyme , is indicated to rapidly lower serum uric acid levels. It converts uric acid into a more soluble metabolite, allantoin, which is easily excreted by the kidneys."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diuretics may also be administered to enhance urinary excretion of electrolytes and prevent renal damage."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prophylaxis for TLS is Allopurinol- it reduces the risk of uric acid nephropathy but is of no benefit once tumor lysis has occurred"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Rasburicase is contraindicated in patients with glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency who are unable to break down hydrogen peroxide, an end product of the urate oxidase reaction. Lysis Syndrome\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"10362\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"1000\"src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/ LhuUEYxLjBRmd3bRZpr71744701330.png\"width=\"667\" />"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management of patients at high risk for tumor lysis syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Intravenous hydration and diuretics (Option A):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"While hydration and diuretics are essential, this option does not address the high uric acid levels or the need for more specific management of tumor lysis syndrome."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Intravenous hydration and calcium gluconate (Option C):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Calcium gluconate is indicated for treating symptomatic hypocalcemia, which can occur due to TLS, but it does not address the underlying metabolic abnormalities."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Intravenous hydration, loop diuretics, and sodium bicarbonate (Option D):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Urinary alkalinization with sodium bicarbonate is no longer recommended. It increases uric acid solubility, but a high pH decreases the solubility of xanthine, hypoxanthine, and calcium phosphate, potentially increasing the likelihood of intratubular crystallization."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Loop diuretics may exacerbate electrolyte imbalances and should be used cautiously."}]},{"n":10,"q":"A 50-year-old male with Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) presents with disease progression while on first-line therapy with imatinib. Molecular testing reveals the presence of the T315I mutation within the ABL kinase domain. What is the most appropriate next step in the management of this patient?","o":[{"t":"Continue imatinib therapy at a higher dose."},{"t":"Switch to a second-generation tyrosine kinase inhibitor (TKI) such as dasatinib or nilotinib."},{"t":"Consider allogeneic hematopoietic stem cell transplantation (allo-HSCT)"},{"t":"Initiate treatment with ponatinib."}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct answer:D) Initiate treatment with ponatinib"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation: The T315I mutation within the ABL kinase domain is a known mechanism of resistance to first-generation tyrosine kinase inhibitors (TKIs) like imatinib."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ponatinib is a third-generation TKI that has demonstrated efficacy against the T315I mutation. It is a suitable treatment option for patients with CML who develop resistance to this specific mutation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Unlike first-generation TKIs such as imatinib, which bind to the ATP-binding site of the BCR-ABL fusion protein, ponatinib is a multi-targeted TKI that also binds to an inactive conformation of BCR-ABL, allowing it to circumvent resistance mechanisms like the T315I mutation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Medical therapeutic options in CML:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"AGENT APPROVED INDICATIO NOTABLE TOXICITIES"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"NS"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Imatinib (Gleevec) All phases Fluid retention, weight gain, nausea,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"diarrhea, skin rashes, periorbital edema, bone or muscle aches and fatigue"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Dasatinib (2nd All phases Myelosuppression, pleural and generation) pericardial effusions , pulmonary"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"hypertension"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Nilotinib (2nd All phases except Diabetes , arterio-occlusive disease , generation) blastic phase pancreatitis"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Bosutinib (2nd All phases Diarrhea , liver toxicity , renal generation) Most effective in case of dysfunction"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"imatinib-resistant CML"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Ponatinib (3rd Optimal TKI if T315I Skin rashes, pancreatitis, generation) mutation arterio-occlusive disease, systemic"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Failure of ‡ 2 TKI’s hypertension"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Omacetaxine Failure of ‡ 2 TKI’s Myelosuppression mepesuccinate (3rd generation)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Continue imatinib therapy at a higher dose (Option A):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"While dose escalation of imatinib may be considered in certain cases of CML with resistance mechanisms other than T315I mutation, it is not typically recommended as the next step in managing T315I-mediated resistance."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Switch to a second-generation tyrosine kinase inhibitor (TKI) such as dasatinib or nilotinib (Option B):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Second-generation TKIs like dasatinib and nilotinib are commonly used as frontline treatments for CML. They are effective against most BCR-ABL mutations, but they have limited activity against the T315I mutation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Switching to a second-generation TKI may be considered if the patient has developed resistance to imatinib due to other mutations or mechanisms."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"However, it may not be effective in cases of the T315I mutation, which specifically confers resistance to these agents."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Consider allogeneic hematopoietic stem cell transplantation (allo-HSCT) (Option C):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Allo-HSCT is a potentially curative treatment for CML."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"However, allo-HSCT is associated with significant risks, including graft-versus-host disease (GVHD) and transplant-related mortality, and is generally reserved for patients with high-risk disease or those who have exhausted other treatment options, including third-generationTKIs."}]},{"n":11,"q":"A 57-year-old male is diagnosed with chronic myeloid leukemia (CML) after presenting with fatigue, night sweats, and splenomegaly. His initial laboratory tests show a white blood cell count of 85,000/µL, hemoglobin of 10.5 g/dL, and a platelet count of 600,000/µL. The oncologist decides to evaluate his prognosis to guide treatment decisions. Which of the following diagnostic tools is most appropriate for assessing the prognosis of this patient with CML?","o":[{"t":"Karnofsky's performance status"},{"t":"Sokal risk score"},{"t":"Ann Arbor staging classification"},{"t":"Rai classification"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct answer: B) Sokal risk score"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation: The Sokal risk score is used to stratify patients with chronic myeloid leukaemia (CML) into risk categories based on prognostic factors at the time of diagnosis. The components include (can be remembered as SPAM ):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"S pleen size."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"P latelet count."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A ge."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"M yeloblast % in the peripheral smear."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"It can be calculated via an online tool by incorporating the values accordingly."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Based on the calculated Sokal score, patients are categorized into risk groups:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Risk Sokal score"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Low <0.8"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Intermediate 0.8-1.2"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"High >1.2"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"High-risk patients have a poor prognosis and should be treated aggressively. Early consideration for allogeneic stem cell transplantation can be done."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The other scoring systems used for the assessment of prognosis in CML are:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hasford score European Treatment and Outcome Study (EUTOS)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Score"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Modification of Sokal score takes into addition two more Two parameters: values: Spleen size. Eosinophil count. Basophil count. Basophil count."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Karnofsky's performance status (Option A) : This tool measures a patient's overall functional status and ability to perform daily activities, but it does not specifically evaluate prognosis in chronic myeloid leukemia (CML)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ann Arbor staging classification (Option C) : This classification is primarily used for staging lymphomas and is not relevant to CML prognosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Rai classification (Option D) : This classification is used for chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL) and does not apply to CML."}]}]);
